  "findings": [
    "The model was trained on 57,319 soldiers, of whom 894 (1.6%) were court-martialled. This severe class imbalance means the model must work hard to distinguish the small minority of court-martialled soldiers from the vast majority who were not.",
    "Feature importance ranking: Enlistment Year (78.7%), Occupation (6.5%), Birth Year (5.9%), Place of Birth (4.2%), Marital Status (3.2%), Rank (1.6%). These percentages indicate how much each feature contributes to the model's splitting decisions. Higher means the feature is more useful for separating court-martialled from non-court-martialled soldiers.",
    "Breaking down by Rank: Corporal had the highest court martial rate at 1.8% (42 of 2,330), while Colonel had the lowest at 0.0% (0 of 96).",
    "Breaking down by Place of Birth: Wales had the highest court martial rate at 4.2% (10 of 238), while Channel Islands had the lowest at 0.0% (0 of 39).",
    "Breaking down by Occupation: Unknown had the highest court martial rate at 2.2% (7 of 362), while Agriculture had the lowest at 1.0% (155 of 14,797).",
    "Breaking down by Marital Status: Not Married had the highest court martial rate at 2.1% (644 of 30,767), while Single had the lowest at 0.6% (91 of 14,640).",
    "For Enlistment Year: soldiers with values ≤ 1916 had a court martial rate of 2.1%, compared to 0.6% for those with values > 1916.",
    "For Birth Year: soldiers with values ≤ 1892 had a court martial rate of 1.8%, compared to 1.3% for those with values > 1892.",
    "The model uses balanced class weighting to counteract the 98% majority class. This boosts recall (the ability to catch court-martialled soldiers) at the cost of precision, meaning more non-court-martialled soldiers get incorrectly flagged. This is a deliberate trade-off so the model does not simply predict 'not court-martialled' for everyone."
  ],
